@*
    One sending source, written the way somebody reads it.

    The name on top and the address beneath, or - when nothing has resolved
    the address yet - the address alone, exactly as every row looked before
    any of this existed. That fallback is the important half: the nightly pass
    fills the cache a few hundred addresses at a time, so a table is a mixture
    for a while, and a row with no name has to look deliberate rather than
    broken.

    The address always stays. It is the identity, it is what somebody pastes
    into a ticket or a firewall, and a table that showed only "Microsoft 365"
    would have thrown away the one thing that is precise.

    The name is for reading and never for judging. Whoever holds an address
    writes its PTR, so recognizing a vendor says who owns the wire and nothing
    about whether the mail is legitimate - every verdict on these rows still
    comes from what was signed and from how many unrelated customers the same
    address was seen against.
*@

@if (Named)
{
    <a class="source-name" href="@Href">@Name</a>
    <code class="source-ip muted">@Address</code>
}
else
{
    <a href="@Href"><code>@Address</code></a>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The address, always shown.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Address { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>What to call it, or the address again when nothing is known.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = "";

    private bool Named => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Name)
        && !string.Equals(Name, Address, StringComparison.Ordinal);

    // Escaped because an IPv6 address is full of colons, which are legal in a
    // path segment but not worth relying on - and because whatever ends up in
    // source_ip came out of somebody else's report.
    private string Href => $"/sources/{Uri.EscapeDataString(Address)}";
}
